$\int_{a}^{b}\dfrac{dQ}{dt}\,dt$ is the **change** in $Q$ from $t=a$ to $t=b$, not the amount of $Q$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$t$};
\draw[-{Stealth}] (0,0) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$\dfrac{dQ}{dt}$};
\fill[gray!35] (1.5,0) -- (1.5,1.3375) -- plot[domain=1.5:4.5, smooth, variable=\x] ({\x},{0.15*\x*\x+1}) -- (4.5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[thick, domain=0:5.5, smooth, variable=\x] plot ({\x},{0.15*\x*\x+1});
\draw[dashed] (1.5,0) -- (1.5,1.3375);
\draw[dashed] (4.5,0) -- (4.5,4.0375);
\node[below] at (1.5,0) {$a$};
\node[below] at (4.5,0) {$b$};
\node[font=\small] at (3.0,0.6) {change in $Q$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

